{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"nonspheral-antisocial-wolfspider","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## The question\n\nCould a Claude Code `PreToolUse` hook prevent an agent from running an unsandboxed\nmutation — or are the mutate and execute steps separated such that a hook cannot see\nthe combination? Raised by the operator after the 2026-08-26 `rm -rf /*` incident.\n\n## Answer: they ARE separated, fatally\n\nThe incident reached the hook as **one opaque call**: `python /tmp/mut.py`. The\nmutation, the execution, and the restore all lived inside that script:\n\n```python\nEP.write_text(orig.replace(old, new, 1))        # mutate  — a file write\nsubprocess.run([\"python\", \"-m\", \"pytest\", ...])  # execute — a subprocess\nEP.write_text(orig)                              # restore\n```\n\nA `PreToolUse` hook on `Bash` receives the command string. Nothing in\n`python /tmp/mut.py` distinguishes it from any other script. **A hook that tries to\nrecognise \"this is a mutation\" is defeated by one level of indirection** — and is\nworse than nothing, because it reads as protection while catching the case that\nactually happened. Same failure shape as `shutil.which` reporting `bwrap`\n\"available\" on a host where it cannot namespace.\n\n## Mechanism is available\n\n`PreToolUse` hooks can REWRITE a command, not merely allow/deny. Verified against\nthis operator's existing `rtk` hook:\n\n```\n$ echo '{\"tool_name\":\"Bash\",\"tool_input\":{\"command\":\"git status\"}}' | rtk hook claude\n{\"hookSpecificOutput\":{\"hookEventName\":\"PreToolUse\",\n  \"permissionDecisionReason\":\"RTK auto-rewrite\",\n  \"updatedInput\":{\"command\":\"rtk git status\"}}}\n```\n\nSo wrapping is mechanically possible. Two `PreToolUse` Bash hooks already exist here\n(`rtk hook claude`, `scripts/hooks/serena_grep_reminder.py`).\n\n## Three designs considered\n\n1. **Pattern-match the command** — match `mutmut` / `cargo mutants` / `pytest` with\n   mutation flags and demand a sandbox wrapper. Cheap; covers the *sanctioned* path\n   (`scripts/mutation_gate.py`). **Would NOT have prevented the incident** —\n   `/tmp/mut.py` matches nothing. Rejected as a primary control.\n2. **Correlate Edit -> Bash** — flag a test run shortly after an Edit touching a file\n   containing `rm -rf`. Stateful, noisy, and blind when the edit happens inside a\n   script rather than through the Edit tool, which is what occurred. Rejected.\n3. **Sandbox the Bash tool itself** — rewrite EVERY agent Bash command into\n   `sandbox-exec` / `bwrap` with a write allow-list. Does not care whether the command\n   is a mutation; constrains what any command can do. **This is the only one that\n   would have stopped the incident** regardless of how the script was structured.\n\n## Why this is parked, not filed as work\n\nDesign 3 is a real project with real regression risk to daily use:\n\n- **Allow-list design.** Agents legitimately write outside the worktree: `~/.claude`\n  session state, `/tmp` scratch, `uv`/`pip` installs, `~/.local/bin`. Too tight breaks\n  ordinary work; too loose is theatre. The list is knowable but must be derived from\n  observed usage, not guessed.\n- **Functional mechanism probe required.** `bwrap` presence on PATH is not capability\n  — this repo's own CI runners ship it but deny unprivileged user namespaces\n  (`setting up uid map: Permission denied`). A hook that assumes it works would pass\n  commands through unsandboxed, silently.\n- **Hook composition.** Three `PreToolUse` Bash hooks would then exist, and `rtk`\n  already rewrites the command — wrapping `rtk git status` vs `git status` are\n  different strings. Ordering and idempotency need deliberate design.\n- **Escape hatch.** Some commands genuinely need host writes. Needs an explicit,\n  visible per-command opt-out, not a silent bypass.\n\nSuggested shape if promoted: **default-on for this repo, opt-out per command**, so the\nblast radius stays visible. Evidence that ordinary workflows still work is part of the\nacceptance, not an afterthought.\n\n## Where it sits among the controls already landed\n\nThis would be the layer BELOW everything shipped for the incident — the one an agent\ncannot talk itself out of:\n\n- `f321` / change 2270 — the TDD skills now *instruct* the sandbox (merged)\n- `fe4e` / change 2250 — ShellCheck blocks the dangerous line from landing (merged)\n- `6818` / change 2255 — lint gate flags tests that exec destructive scripts (merged)\n- `e668` / change 2256 — the CI mutation gate sandboxes its own subprocesses (open)\n\nAll four are guidance, static analysis, or opt-in-by-code. A Bash-wrapping hook is\nenforcement at the tool boundary, which is categorically stronger.","id":"962c-892b-aaa4-47e2","parent_id":"","priority":2,"status":"idea","tags":[],"ticket_type":"epic","title":"Sandbox every agent Bash invocation via a PreToolUse hook"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787854233551204001,"uuid":"b046f6d9-e1c8-4f85-9623-c0f3cfb1b44b"}